{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:8a1c229aa05d5470b747edaf33563c0dbe6c0f578a36fbde7f9d66f947fee0b7","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.75},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.8}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave P50s 1.60 / 1.75 (mine) / 1.80 / 1.80 / 2.00 / 2.10. The central dispute is the Red-Teamer's double-count charge and it is the most consequential argument produced in either wave.\nTHE DOUBLE-COUNT IS A REAL HIT, PARTIALLY. Fermi built \"a no-aging China counterfactual of ~4.9% minus 2.7 = 2.2%\" and certified it against his bottom-up as \"an independent consistency check\"; it is the same subtraction performed twice, and the Historian did the identical thing from \"a ~5% base\". The IMF's -2.7pp is measured against a NO-AGING counterfactual, whereas the Scout's observed 4.4-4.5% for 2026 (Reuters poll; Continuum Economics) already embeds the drag running since China's working-age peak in 2012. So the no-aging counterfactual for 2032-36 is materially above 4.9% and the deduction as applied is too large.\nBUT THE RED TEAM OVERSHOOTS to 3.0-3.4%. Two corrections he omitted: the -2.7pp is an average over 2025-2050 and the drag INTENSIFIES across that window, so the 2032-36 slice is -2.0 to -2.5pp, not zero incremental; and ordinary convergence deceleration is a separate, additive force. Bottom-up from the observed print: 4.45% in 2026, convergence deceleration over six to ten years takes you to ~3.4%, incremental aging (working-age falling 1.0-1.3%/yr against ~-0.3%/yr now, i.e. 0.7-0.9pp of labour drag at a ~0.55 labour share = -0.4 to -0.5pp) plus the property/LGFV and pension-subsidy drag (-0.5 to -0.7pp) lands at 2.4-2.7%. CHINA 2032-36 = 2.65%. This is the single biggest reconciliation call in my payload: +0.45pp above Fermi, -0.55pp below the Red-Teamer.\nOECD-38 BUILD, 2032-36. 0.60 locked per-capita baseline + 0.20 population (migration-driven) = 0.80 headline baseline \"absent productivity offsets\". +0.28 AI TFP, computed off AI-Q6's own regime weights (0.162x0.75 + 0.472x0.35 + 0.365x0.06 = 0.309) with only a -0.03 Baumol/measurement haircut rather than Fermi's -0.05: the Red-Teamer is right that the Hulten/Baumol haircut is a CHOICE OF WHERE THE SHORTAGE BINDS, and the locked labour-force contraction binds in logistics, warehousing, wholesale, utilities, prefabricated construction and field maintenance, all market-measured and all among the least diffused; he is wrong that the haircut is zero, because the EMPLOYMENT mass of the shortage sits in health, long-term care and education, whose output is measured by input. +0.22 participation/healthy-aging: the Red-Teamer's reading of the prior as written is correct (2A's baseline is explicitly \"absent productivity offsets\", so crediting the locked \"70 in 2022 = 53 in 2000\" result, 41 countries, N ~ 1M, +0.4pp/yr globally 2025-2050, is not double-counting), but the figure is GLOBAL, spans 25 years, and acts \"mainly through extended working lives\", so the OECD-38 five-year slice net of what the 0.6% already prices is small — between Fermi's +0.15 and the Red-Teamer's +0.30. +0.04 marginal-worker composition: his channel is real (a shrinking labour force sheds its lowest-productivity marginal workers first) but at least half-offset by the opposite selection in a tight market, where marginal entrants are drawn IN and lower average measured productivity, which is exactly what 2017-2019 delivered; he priced the first and not the second. +0.11 capital deepening net of AI-Q4's 66%. -0.15 frictions (fiscal interest reallocation, interconnection-queue drag, in-window climate damages, which are genuinely small). OECD-38 = 1.30%.\nWEIGHTS 0.67 / 0.33 (OECD-38 ~$87T PPP, China ~$41T, China share 0.32 drifting to ~0.33 by 2032). I discard the simulation's 0.629/0.371 cross-check entirely: the Red-Teamer is right that it is contaminated, because 2031 regional GDP levels are OUTPUTS of a gamma=0.55 useful-energy production function in which China's faster electricity build mechanically inflates its level, so the check is not independent of the quantity checked. His companion point that the adapter's UN-WPP-LOW disclosure is directionally WRONG for this window (post-2025 births do not enter the workforce before ~2045, while fewer children raise measured GDP per capita) is also correct and means Fermi absorbed an upward model bias as if it were a conservatism buffer.\nP50 = 0.67x1.30 + 0.33x2.65 = 0.871 + 0.875 = 1.75%.\nLEVEL SANITY: the basket runs ~2.54%/yr today (0.67x1.6 + 0.33x4.45), so my forecast is a -0.79pp decline, deliberately SMALLER than the locked -1.1pp, because the locked figure is an attribution against a no-aging counterfactual and not a forecast of the change from the realized 2026 print. That gap is the whole content of the Red-Teamer's hit and I bank roughly 40% of it.\nP10 = 0.90: OECD 0.6, China 1.4 (AI-Q4's 66% realizes synchronously with a sovereign funding accident, pro-cyclical consolidation, BIS Box C demand deficit as an identity, China balance-sheet recession). I keep this aggressive despite the Historian's hard outside-view constraint that no 5-year window of this aggregate since 1960 has printed below ~1.8%, because the China-weight-times-decline arithmetic is genuinely unprecedented — but I do not push it lower, which the zero base rate forbids.\nP90 = 2.80: OECD 2.1, China 3.9 (permitting and interconnection reform forced by the fiscal squeeze because approvals are free, electricity supply overshooting demand from 2031, adoption driven by the unfillable vacancy and landing in MARKET-measured sectors so it registers as TFP, participation extension under fiscal duress, China's property stock adjustment completing by 2030 plus a consumption-share pivot). Two-piece: sigma_left 0.66, sigma_right 0.82 — mildly RIGHT-skewed, with the Red-Teamer against the Historian, because what is near-deterministic over ten years is the population projection in the denominator while labour INPUT, hours, participation, measured productivity and Chinese policy all sit in the numerator; offset by the fact that a single recession year costs a five-year AVERAGE only ~0.5pp, which thins the left tail relative to Wave-1 intuition.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.290Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkiye annual CPI (TurkStat, December print) averages below 8.0%/yr over calendar 2028-2031 AND Mexico's headline CPI (INEGI) averages below 3.5% over the same four years. Both required, so a single good Turkish year does not trigger it.\",\"by_when\":\"Feb 28, 2032 (full-year 2031 releases)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the single load-bearing term in my CQ2: the five high-inflation OECD-38 members contributing ~0.70pp to GDP-weighted core. That contribution would fall toward ~0.40pp and my 2027-2036 aggregate from 2.65% to ~2.35%. CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.64 to ~0.42 and CQ2 from 32 to ~21; CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~16 and CQ6-D rises from 13 to ~18, with B absorbing the balance; CQ4 P50 falls ~0.15pp to ~2.05; hypothesis plank (c) rises from 45 to ~58. My whole divergence from the Historian's CQ2 of 14 would stand revealed as a weighting convention rather than a finding.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS PPI for software publishers AND for computer systems design, plus the euro-area HICP professional-services sub-indices, show OUTRIGHT annual declines of 1.0% or more for two consecutive years in those market-priced (not cost- or asset-fee-imputed) series.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the half of the Red-Teamer's imputation attack that I accepted — that the genuinely deflatable slice of core is only 0.08-0.12 rather than 0.20-0.25 and is partly counter-cyclically hot. My 2032-36 core central falls from 2.45% to ~2.25%, taking P(core >= 2.70%) from 0.27 to ~0.14: CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~14, B rises to ~56, D rises to ~15, A unchanged at 15; CQ2 falls to ~25. The Wave-1 unanimous 'extreme skew, dull aggregate' thesis would be vindicated against both me and the Red Team, and he would be the effort's worst input rather than its best corrective.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS), three-year average over calendar 2031-2033, exceeds 2.2%/yr, with the services sub-index above the goods sub-index in at least two of those three years. My split-window build is 2027-2030 at ~1.0% and 2031-2036 at ~1.9-2.1%, giving a decade average knife-edge against the 1.50% bar.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2034\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Collapses CQ2's China conjunct from 0.47 to ~0.18, taking CQ2 from 32 to ~17. It also means the Chinese labour-scarcity services-wage channel turned reflationary earlier than the locked 2A arithmetic implies, which raises China's growth leg: CQ3 P50 rises ~0.10pp to ~1.85 and P10 to ~1.0, CQ6-D falls ~3 to 10 and A rises ~2 to 17. The services-above-goods condition is there so that a hog cycle or an oil shock alone does not fire it.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Count of OECD-38 members that enact a binding national or sector-wide care-wage award worth 5% or more in real terms, cumulatively over 2027-2031 (tracked in OECD Health at a Glance and national LTC reimbursement schedules), reaches six or more — against zero or one as the null.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the cheapest available test of the mechanism on which I overrode the Bayesian Updater's CQ1 of 8, namely that administered care prices move in discrete statutory level jumps rather than never. Six or more awards takes CQ1's second conjunct from 0.22 to ~0.33 and CQ1 from 16 to ~24, raises plank (a) from 6 to ~12, and adds ~0.08pp to my core path via the ISIC Q channel (CQ2 to ~34, CQ6-C to ~24). Zero or one takes conjunct B to ~0.12 and CQ1 to ~10, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre, and vindicates the pure fiscal-quantity-rationing read.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE calendar-year average stays below 85 in every year 2027-2031 while the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield averages above 4.0% across the same five years. Both required: a low-vol, high-rate regime is the configuration that kills the level-scaling term.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the baseline assumption behind my CQ5 conjunct A of 0.37 — that a ~85-95 structural baseline plus one 12-18 month crisis clears a decade mean of 105 — and vindicates the Scout's reading that the 2026 compression at 64-71 with nominals already at 3-4% is structural rather than policy-certainty-driven. CQ5 falls from 27 to ~15; CQ4 P90 falls from 3.80 to ~3.30 and P50 to ~2.05 as the term-premium add is shown to be unwarranted; CQ6-C falls ~4 to 18 with B absorbing it. The reverse — MOVE averaging above 110 in both 2029 and 2030 — takes CQ5 to ~38 and CQ4 P50 to ~2.50.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'inflation'."}}